From afcfb434f2ead1165e6a23ca9828c3cd414b1f21 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MT6762 local experiment <mt6762-build@localhost>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 23:14:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm: add opt-in MT6762 I2C DMA laboratory fixtures

AI-assisted local research code, not intended for upstream submission. Model recovered controller registers with explicitly synthetic peripherals, fault injection and bounded QTest coverage.
---
 docs/system/arm/mt6762.rst      |  69 +++++
 hw/arm/meson.build              |   2 +-
 hw/arm/mt6762.c                 |  84 +++++-
 hw/arm/mt6762_lab_i2c.c         | 460 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 include/hw/arm/mt6762_lab_i2c.h |  12 +
 tests/mt6762/test_lab_i2c.py    | 192 +++++++++++++
 6 files changed, 816 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 hw/arm/mt6762_lab_i2c.c
 create mode 100644 include/hw/arm/mt6762_lab_i2c.h
 create mode 100755 tests/mt6762/test_lab_i2c.py

diff --git a/docs/system/arm/mt6762.rst b/docs/system/arm/mt6762.rst
index ed75c6a..cb9a0df 100644
--- a/docs/system/arm/mt6762.rst
+++ b/docs/system/arm/mt6762.rst
@@ -76,3 +76,72 @@ under ``chosen`` or ``chosen@0``; there is no device-tree endian conversion.
 signature 0xabcd1234, and returns an error when no valid old log exists.
 This is a bootloader handoff model; the source Image and extracted DTB files
 remain unchanged, and the guest DTB is edited by QEMU's boot helper.
+
+Opt-in native-driver I2C laboratory
+----------------------------------
+
+``-M mt6762,lab-i2c=on`` adds synthetic fixtures for testing native driver
+control flow. With the property omitted, these MMIO regions and devices
+remain absent. This does not change the documented stock-kernel boundary.
+The fixtures implement the recovered register interface, not physical phone
+behavior or proof of native hardware compatibility:
+
+* I2C0 at 0x11007000 / APDMA at 0x11000080 / SPI84; I2C5 at 0x11016000 /
+  APDMA at 0x11000580 / SPI130. Controller accesses are 16-bit, DMA accesses
+  32-bit. Single reads/writes and write/repeated-START/read are supported.
+* FIFO capacity is eight bytes. DMA accepts at most 4096 bytes per direction,
+  checks 33-bit addresses and requires the entire buffer to be guest RAM.
+  Shared TX/RX buffers are supported. Invalid mappings/lengths fail without
+  publishing receive bytes. There is no IOMMU or cache-coherency simulation.
+* Peripheral interrupt status is W1C, routed to the real GIC model; masking
+  controls assertion. A transfer completes after a fixed synthetic 100us,
+  unrelated to SCL programming. Timing/divider register values are retained
+  for driver readback; this is not electrical/timing validation.
+* ``infracfg_ao`` at 0x10001000 implements active-high-disable I2C AP gate
+  bank0/bit11 and APDMA gate bank1/bit18, their SET/CLR/status registers.
+  Both begin disabled. ``topckgen`` at 0x10000000 exposes I2C mux bits2:0 and
+  gate7 at offset0xa0, SET0xa4/CLR0xa8. Parent0 is the 26MHz fixture; other
+  parents stall transfers because their PLLs are not modeled. Losing a
+  required clock during a transfer leaves it incomplete.
+* The read-only GPIO fixture at 0x10005000 exposes mode1 for pins82/83 and
+  pins48/49, at mode offsets0x3a0 and0x360. No electrical pin state or GPIO
+  write/power sequencing is modeled.
+* Synthetic AXS at bus0/address0x3b returns bytes00,07 to write0x0c/read2.
+  A direct32-byte read returns a zero-contact frame by default. Explicit
+  ``lab-touch-sequence=on`` cycles16 down/move frames (ID0, coordinates
+  100..115/200..215) then48 zero-contact release frames. These are test
+  vectors, not captured touches. The cadence is driven by read count.
+* Synthetic PSC5425E at bus5/address0x6a has identity0xf0, status0x10 and
+  control0. Seven byte registers support bounded repeated-START reads;
+  registers other than identity accept test writes. Setting control bit2 clears
+  status bits5:4 in the deterministic disable fixture. No battery or charging
+  physics is inferred. A lab-only scratch peripheral at address0x50 on
+  both buses provides a256-byte modulo-indexed memory for boundary tests.
+
+The QOM properties ``lab-touch-sequence`` and ``lab-i2c-fault`` can be changed
+through ``qom-set`` on ``/machine``. Faults are ``none``, ``nack``, ``dma``
+(simultaneous COMPLETE+DMAERR) or ``stall`` (START and DMA ENABLE remain set).
+Changing a fault back to ``none`` does not resume an already-stalled transfer.
+Controller soft reset cancels work but does not reset DMA ENABLE; APDMA reset
+is separate. This intentionally exercises callers' bounded waits and pinned
+DMA failure state. ``lab-i2c`` itself is immutable after initialization.
+
+Run the independent, bounded QTest MMIO checks::
+
+  python3 tests/mt6762/test_lab_i2c.py --qemu build/qemu-system-aarch64 \
+    --dtb /path/to/extracted/boot.bin.dtb
+
+The tests cover default absence, clock gating, FIFO/repeated START, GIC/W1C,
+PSC fixture access, FIFO overflow, DMA length/range boundaries, buffers above
+4GiB, canaries, synthetic contact/release frames, injected errors, stalled
+DMA recovery and mid-transfer clock loss. Passing them establishes internal
+model behavior only. Native XNU execution is a separate integration test;
+physical-device validation remains separate from both.
+
+Read-only QOM property ``lab-i2c-state`` returns JSON counters for each bus:
+START transactions, successful DMA reads, successful receive bytes and last
+terminal status. This lets a native guest integration probe distinguish a
+worker's startup message from actual completed DMA, and record failure
+injection separately. These counters describe only the synthetic model.
+The bus5 counter record also exposes the synthetic PSC control/status bytes,
+so integration evidence can establish CE-bit readback after a guest write.
